WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.620
The way you set up your training,

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:04.540
dev or development sets and test sets

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.500
can have a huge impact on how rapidly you or your team

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.140
can make progress on building a machine learning application.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.900
But seeing teams, even teams in very large companies,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.540
set up these data sets in ways that really slows down

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.280
rather than speeds up the progress of the team.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.940
Let's take a look at how you can set up these data sets

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.560
to maximize your team's efficiency.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.460
In this video, I wanna focus on how you set up

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.540
your dev and test sets.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.100
So the dev set is also called the development set

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.540
or sometimes called the holdout cross validation set.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.860
And workflow in machine learning is that

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.300
you try a lot of ideas, train up different models

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.660
on the training set and then use the dev set

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.420
to evaluate the different ideas and pick one

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.700
and keep iterating to improve dev set performance

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.100
until finally you have one class value happy with

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.500
that you then evaluate on your test set.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.500
Now, let's say by way of example that you're building

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.980
a cat classifier and you are operating in these regions.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.300
In the US, UK, other European countries,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.460
South America, India, China, other Asian countries

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.680
and Australia.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.340
So how do you set up your dev set and your test set?

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.260
Well, one way you could do so is to pick four

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.380
of these regions, I'm gonna use these four

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.820
but it could be four randomly chosen regions

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.580
and say that data from these four regions

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.700
will go into the dev set and the other four regions,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:28.660
I'm just gonna use these four

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.740
but we've randomly chosen four as well

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.380
that those will go into the test set.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.880
It turns out this is a very bad idea

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.220
because in this example, your dev and test sets

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.820
come from different distributions.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.420
I would instead recommend that you find a way

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.260
to make your dev and test sets come

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.700
from the same distribution.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.680
So here's what I mean.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.660
One picture to keep in mind is that I think

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.500
setting up your dev set plus your single row number

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.780
evaluation metric, that's like placing a target

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.580
and telling your team where you think

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.700
is the bullseye you want to aim at.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.540
Because what happened once you've established

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.780
the dev set in the metric is that the team

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:09.980
can iterate very quickly, try out different ideas,

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:12.980
run experiments and very quickly use the dev set

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.980
and the metric to evaluate classifiers

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.540
and try to pick the best one.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.900
So machine learning teams are often very good

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.860
at shooting different arrows at the target

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.220
and iterating to get closer and closer

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.660
to hitting the bullseye,

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.060
to doing well on your metric on your dev set.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.300
And the problem with how we've set up the dev

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.060
and test set in the example on the left

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.260
is that your team might spend months

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.440
iterating to do well on the dev set

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.220
only to realize that when you finally go

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:41.740
to test and launch test set,

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.020
that data from these four countries

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.420
or these four regions at the bottom

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.460
might be very different than the regions in the dev set.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.940
So you might have a nasty surprise

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.900
and realize that all the months of work

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.760
you spend optimizing to the dev set

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.900
is not giving you good performance on the test set.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.820
So having dev and test sets from different distributions

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.400
is like setting a target,

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.740
having your team spend months

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.700
trying to aim closer and closer to the bullseye

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.480
only to realize after months of work

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.600
that you say, oh wait, to test it,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.800
I'm gonna move the target over here.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.240
And the team might say, well,

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.580
why did you make us spend months

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.420
optimizing for a different bullseye

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.740
when suddenly you can move the bullseye

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.060
to a different location somewhere else?

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.140
So to avoid this, what I recommend instead

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.780
is that you take all this data

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.060
and randomly shuffle the data into the dev and test set

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.900
so that both the dev and test sets

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.940
have data from all eight regions

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.900
and that the dev and test sets

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.240
really come from the same distribution,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.220
which is the distribution of all of your data

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.900
mixed together.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.100
Here's another example.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.660
This is actually a true story,

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.220
but with some details changed.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:47.460
So I know a machine learning team,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.220
they actually spent several months

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.060
optimizing on a dev set,

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.260
which was comprised of loan approvals

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.380
for medium income zip codes.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.000
So the specific machine learning problem

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.860
was given input X about a loan application,

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.300
can you predict Y,

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.900
which is whether or not they'll be paid alone.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.100
So this helps you decide whether or not to approve a loan.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.380
And so the dev sets came from loan applications,

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.740
they came from medium income zip codes.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.540
Zip codes is what we call postal codes

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.060
in the United States.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.460
But after working on this for a few months,

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:21.140
the team then suddenly decided to test this

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.660
on data from low income zip codes

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.420
or low income postal codes.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:26.260
And of course,

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.500
the distribution of data from medium income

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.900
and low income zip codes is very different.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.380
And the classifier that they spent so much time optimizing

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.060
in the former case just didn't work well at all

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:39.500
on the latter case.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.940
And so this particular team actually wasted about

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.660
three months of time

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.500
and had to go back and really redo a lot of work.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.780
And what happened here was the team spent

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.260
three months aiming for one target.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.140
And then after three months,

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.300
the manager asked,

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.820
oh, how are you doing on hitting this other target?

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.420
This is a totally different location.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.520
And it just was a very frustrating experience for the team.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.780
So what I recommend for set up a dev set and test set is

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.180
choose a dev set and test set

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.060
to reflect data you expect to get in the future

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.500
and consider important to do well on.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:12.780
And in particular,

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.020
the dev set and the test set here

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:20.420
should come from the same distribution.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.860
So whatever type of data you expect to get in the future

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.760
and want to do well on,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.740
try to get data that looks like that.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:29.220
And whatever that data is,

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.720
put it into both your dev set and your test set.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.660
Because that way you're putting the target

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.020
where you actually want to hit

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.540
and having the team iterate very efficiently

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.740
to hitting that same target,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.820
hopefully the same target as well.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:44.180
You notice we haven't talked yet

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.340
about how to set up a training set.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:48.940
We'll talk about the training set in a later video,

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:50.740
but the important takeaway from this video

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:54.020
is that setting up the dev set

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.860
as well as the evaluation metric

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.780
is really defining what target you want to aim at.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.900
And hopefully by setting the dev set and the test set

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:04.340
to the same distribution,

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.380
you're really aiming at whatever target

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.220
you hope your machine learning team will hit.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:11.540
The way you choose your training set

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.580
will affect how well you can actually hit that target,

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:18.100
but we can talk about that separately in a later video.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.820
So I know some machine learning teams

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.020
that could literally have saved themselves months of work

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:23.980
or they follow the guidelines in this video.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.260
So I hope these guidelines will help you too.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:29.900
Next, it turns out that the size of your dev and test sets,

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:31.140
how to choose the size of them

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.560
is also changing in the era of deep learning.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:35.599
Let's talk about that in the next video.
